query. The repo has no schema or index definitions, so the index advice assumes the usual layout (`users.id` is the primary key, and there are no indexes on `users.email` or `orders.user_id`).\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The database evaluates `lower()` on every row. This is the main cost.\n2. **Full scan of `orders`, or a hash join that reads all of it.** Without an index on `orders.user_id`, the join can't look up one user's orders. The planner has to scan `orders` or build a hash table over it. Cost grows with the size of `orders`, even though the filter matches about one user.\n3. **Heap fetches and extra I/O from `SELECT *`.** It returns every column from both tables, including the duplicate `id` and `user_id` columns. It also blocks index-only scans and sends more data to the client than needed.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**Index for the email lookup.** Pick one of these:\n\n```sql\n-- Option A: expression index, keeps the query as written\nCREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n```\n\n```sql\n-- Option B: normalize at write time and drop lower() from the query\n-- Postgres: use the citext type or a CHECK (email = lower(email)) constraint\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_users_email ON users (email);\n-- query: WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'\n```\n\nOption B is cleaner. It makes the lookup a plain index seek, and a unique index also enforces one account per email. If you can't change how emails are stored, use option A. In MySQL, the default case-insensitive collation already makes `lower()` unnecessary. In SQL Server, the same applies to the default collation.\n\n**Index for the join.**\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nIf you usually sort or filter orders by date, use `(user_id, created_at)` instead.\n\n**Select only the columns you need.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.status, o.total, o.created_at, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE u.email = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\nIf the query is used for pagination, add `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT n`. A user with many orders would otherwise return an unbounded result set.\n\n## Expected result\n\nWith both indexes, the plan should be an index seek on `users` (one row), then an index lookup on `orders.user_id`. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after to confirm. I couldn't run it here because there's no database connection or schema in the repo.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}